======================================================================================
1. Files in this repository derived from other projects
======================================================================================

rules/reserved/emilys-modifiers.py (and rules/reserved/emilys-modifiers.json, generated from it)
    Derived from "Emily's Modifier Dictionary" (https://github.com/EPLHREU/emily-modifiers, file
    emily-modifiers.py), by way of the copy shipped in Lapwing's plover-lapwing-aio
    (https://github.com/aerickt/plover-lapwing-aio, plover_lapwing/dictionaries/emily-modifiers.py),
    whose ender "LGTS" this file keeps. The file's own header says it was forked from
    steno/disabled_20-lapwing-extras/ (an older Lapwing path, not checked).
    License of the upstream: the EPLHREU/emily-modifiers repository has no LICENSE file, its README
    states no license and GitHub reports none: no license is granted that could be read. The
    plover-lapwing-aio repository is MIT (copy below), which covers its own authors' work only.
    The author's rights in Emily's file are unverified; ask the author if in doubt.

rules/reserved/punctuation.json
    The Reference describes it as forked from Plover's punctuation strokes (Plover is
    https://github.com/openstenoproject/plover; its repository LICENSE.txt is GNU GPL version 2,
    and setup.cfg says "GPL-2.0-or-later"). Which entries are copied and which are lyk's own is
    not recorded in the repository: unverified. Copyright line seen in Plover's source:
    "Copyright (c) 2010-2011 Joshua Harlan Lifton" (plover/__init__.py, "See LICENSE.txt").

rules/reserved/commands.json
    Uses Plover's command syntax ({PLOVER:...}, {#key}); the strokes are lyk's. No copied
    Plover file is recorded: unverified.

rules/plover.toml
    A transcription of Plover theory's sound-to-key assignments as rules (the notation and the
    layout are Plover's). It holds no file text from Plover: unverified whether more than that.

Everything else under src/, rules/ (the other files), tools/, site/ and build.rs has no recorded
upstream. rules/reserved/sticky-plus.rs and rules/reserved/regenerate.py are written for lyk.
The Cargo dependencies are listed in section 4.

======================================================================================
2. Compatibility with GPL-3.0-or-later (facts, not conclusions)
======================================================================================

 - Plover: GPL-2.0-or-later per its setup.cfg; "or later" permits GPL-3.0. Its LICENSE.txt is the
   GPL version 2 text, whose "any later version" wording is in the file. The Plover dictionaries
   lyk downloads (section 3) are in that repository.
 - Lapwing (plover-lapwing-aio): MIT, a permissive license, one-way compatible with GPL-3.0.
   Lapwing's book repository (aerickt/lapwing-for-beginners) reports GPL-3.0; lyk uses neither.
 - Emily's Modifier Dictionary: no license found, so compatibility cannot be confirmed:
   unverified, and this is the one open item.

======================================================================================
3. Data `lyk fetch` downloads (src/fetch.rs)
======================================================================================

Nothing below is shipped in this repository: `lyk fetch` downloads it into the data folder. The
dictionary `lyk build` generates is derived from it (pronunciations, word frequencies, the
Plover/Lapwing/other-theory dictionaries it is compared and merged with). What follows is what the
upstream states; how it applies to the generated dictionary is not decided here.

 Pronunciations
 - CMUdict (cmusphinx/cmudict): "Copyright (C) 1993-2015 Carnegie Mellon University", a
   redistribution license keeping the copyright notice and disclaimer (LICENSE read; notice below).
 - MFA US English dictionary v3.1.0 (MontrealCorpusTools/mfa-models): CC BY 4.0 (the release page
   states it; the repository LICENSE is CC BY 4.0).
 - WikiPron US English broad (CUNY-CL/wikipron): the code is Apache-2.0; its README says the
   Wiktionary data has "its own licensing terms" (Wiktionary:Copyrights), not read further here.
 Frequencies and text
 - FrequencyWords en_full.txt (hermitdave/FrequencyWords, from OpenSubtitles 2018): README says MIT
   for code and CC-BY-SA-4.0 for content.
 - Norvig's count_2w.txt (https://www.norvig.com/ngrams/): license unverified (not read).
 - Project Gutenberg books (Pride and Prejudice, Huckleberry Finn, A Tale of Two Cities, Moby Dick,
   Walden; src/corpus_extract.rs): public-domain works by the authors; the Gutenberg license text
   itself was not read here: unverified. Used only by `score`'s benchmark corpus.
 Dictionaries of other theories (a pool of outlines the generator weighs)
 - Plover main.json and commands.json (openstenoproject/plover): GPL-2.0 repository, see above.
 - Lapwing dictionaries (aerickt/plover-lapwing-aio): MIT, Copyright (c) 2024 Aerick (below).
 - Stan's dictionaries (stanographer/steno-dictionaries): no license file; the README's License
   section reads "Don't care.": not a formal license, unverified.
 - Josiah theory (Josiah-tan/Josiah_theory): no license file or statement found: unverified.
 - Thirteen Stenovations theories via user202729/plover-resources: no license file or statement
   found, and each theory's own authors' terms are unknown: unverified.
 Run time only (not fetched by `lyk fetch`)
 - `lyk quote` asks https://zenquotes.io/api/random for a quote (src/quote.rs): its terms were
   not read: unverified.

